Differential-Resolution Theory 
By Claudette Raynor 

Differential Resolution Theory of Meaning (DRT): A Unified Framework for Relational Intelligence
By Claudette Raynor

Abstract
Complex reasoning requires a problem state that holds still while candidate interpretations are tested against it. Without such a state there is nothing to test against, and evaluation of one candidate displaces the reference that the next candidate would need. This paper introduces the Differential Resolution Theory of Meaning (DRT), which treats that requirement as architectural rather than biological: it is a condition on any system resolving relational ambiguity, and different substrates satisfy it in different ways.
Reinterpreting Global Workspace Theory, which historically characterized working memory as an illuminated stage for conscious awareness, DRT holds that the workspace is constituted by mutual conditioning rather than located anatomically. It forms wherever unresolved predictive mismatch sustains reentrant density, and it dissolves when that mismatch falls below the threshold required for action. The biological implementation is transient by necessity: accumulation builds a configuration, reentrant signaling stabilizes it enough to be read without being rewritten, the stabilized configuration is what becomes available as the resolution, and decay in the participating populations ends it. A synthetic system with a dedicated intermediate representation satisfies the same requirement without rebuilding the space each time.
This framing is testable against existing lesion and stimulation evidence. Damage to prefrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and the thalamic nuclei supporting reentrant connectivity produces a specific dissociation: knowledge is retained, single-constraint resolution survives, and multi-step conflict resolution fails. That is the signature predicted by a continually reformed holding space whose reformation has been disrupted.
Bridging biophysical thermodynamics and selectional neurobiology with psychological cognitive dissonance, relational semiotics, and artificial neural architectures, the theory shows that conceptual meaning is a metastable equilibrium of predictive tension. Meaning is settled pragmatically, just enough to cross operational thresholds and permit action within environmental limits, leaving a lingering trace that remains top of mind until displaced by new context or acute tension. The same dynamic appears at collective scale, in human groups and now in populations of artificial agents, where recent multiagent research documents both its successes and its characteristic failures.


Core Premise
For decades, cognitive science operated under the assumption that human consciousness arises when information is broadcast across distributed brain networks (Baars, 1988). Classical Global Workspace Theory formalized this view using a theater metaphor, framing working memory as an illuminated stage where broadcast constitutes conscious awareness. Contemporary research has pushed the field further. Dehaene and Changeux’s Global Neuronal Workspace introduced a dynamic ignition model centered on access, and Whyte and Smith (2021) expanded these concepts computationally by formulating a predictive global neuronal workspace that merges GWT with active inference.
Differential Resolution Theory reframes the premise. The workspace is not a stage for subjective awareness. It is holding infrastructure that allows a processing system, biological or synthetic, to evaluate relationships, resolve conceptual ambiguity, and orient itself within a shifting environment.
Any system that reasons across multiple steps must be able to return to the same target after each evaluation. If the act of testing a candidate modifies the state the candidate was tested against, the second test is run against something different from the first, and composition fails. A held state is one that can be read without being written to, and that survives the reading. This is the property DRT identifies as necessary, and it is a property a system either has or lacks regardless of what it is made of.
Understanding the biological case requires abandoning the idea of a rigid or static mental container. Grounding working memory architecture in contemporary models of attentional focus (Oberauer, 2002; Lewis-Peacock et al., 2012), what occupies the workspace is not passive storage but a fluid reference frame: a target concept, goal, or problem state held in context while distributed sub-networks run candidate evaluations against it. These background networks feed back into the active reference frame, gradually nudging and updating its orientation until an acceptable alignment is reached.
This architecture replaces Global Workspace Theory’s traditional winner-takes-all endpoint with pragmatic functional alignment. Classical models of cognitive conflict assume competing representations battle until a single definitive winner occupies the workspace. Decades of formal computational modeling demonstrate instead that systems stop at good enough. In bounded accumulation frameworks (Ratcliff, 1978) and satisficing models (Simon, 1956), processing terminates once a decision criterion is met.
DRT formalizes this pragmatic stopping rule through the thermodynamic and predictive mechanics of error minimization. As the action-based model of cognitive dissonance establishes (Harmon-Jones et al., 2009), an active organism or autonomous agent cannot remain trapped in infinite comparison, because it faces physical deadlines, computational limits, and survival mandates. By synthesizing thermodynamic energy constraints with operational limits, DRT holds that processing halts as soon as variational free energy drops below an operational action threshold, allowing action to occur. The threshold is not defined by the stopping it explains. In bounded accumulation it is set by the cost of delay weighed against the cost of error (Ratcliff, 1978; Simon, 1956), both specifiable independently of when any particular decision happens to terminate.
Because pragmatic resolution rarely drops predictive tension to absolute zero, residual dissonance remains in the system. This residual trace is what keeps the active reference frame available in the workspace. It explains the persistence phenomena first studied by Zeigarnik (1927): interrupted tasks and unresolved conceptual frames remain accessible, staying active to guide ongoing interpretation, emotional appraisal, and downstream processing until incoming context, task demands, or a more acute predictive tension displaces them.
This reframing displaces traditional notions of conscious awareness. What cognitive science historically mislabeled as conscious awareness is not an illuminated stage or an executive controller. It is the felt operational signal of unresolved predictive dissonance generated while background channels evaluate options against an active reference frame. Dissonance before resolution is not perceived; the evaluation itself runs unconsciously. What is perceived is the resolution. Once predictive dissonance drops below the threshold required for action, the operational signal subsides, which explains why highly fluent, automated operations execute smoothly without subjective awareness or computational overhead.

Building a Bridge from Biophysics to Semiotics
DRT grounds its architecture on a continuous physical and conceptual bridge connecting selectional neuroscience, biophysical thermodynamics, psychological cognition, and relational semiotics. Rather than treating the mind as an isolated machine processing static rules, the framework recognizes living cognition as an adaptive biological ecosystem that continuously self-organizes to preserve organizational integrity across changing environments.
Gerald Edelman established that biological development and learning proceed through somatic selection rather than central executive control (Edelman, 1987). Through Neural Darwinism, the brain generates extensive structural variation among its neuronal groups, which compete for functional reinforcement based on experiential value and internal feedback. This selective dynamic is driven by two structural features: reentrant signaling, the continuous parallel non-linear signal exchange between interconnected brain maps, and structural degeneracy, which enables non-identical biological structures to execute equivalent functions. Together, reentry and degeneracy allow distributed regions to coordinate complex activity without a centralized supervisory controller. DRT takes this further than Edelman did. Where Edelman retained consciousness as something the selectional system produces, DRT holds that the way the selection system coheres is itself what the term consciousness has been pointing at. A second difference should be marked. Edelman and Tononi's dynamic core is constituted by reentrant thalamocortical activity and is explicitly not a place, which is the lineage of the identity argument used here. But the core is in continuous flux and reconstituted moment to moment, and Edelman never claimed it holds a configuration stable enough to be consulted without being reshaped. That stability is this framework's addition to his architecture, and the burden of supporting it sits here rather than with him.
Karl Friston provided the thermodynamic formalization for why this selectional process takes place by introducing the Free Energy Principle (Friston, 2010). Self-organizing living systems persist against physical entropy by minimizing variational free energy, a mathematical upper bound on statistical surprise and expectation mismatch. Friston demonstrated that Edelman’s concept of optimizing somatic value is mathematically equivalent to minimizing prediction error. Within DRT, Edelman’s neuronal group selection represents the physical substrate through which an organism continuously adapts its internal representations and active engagements to minimize thermodynamic error over time.
Roope Kaaronen bridged this biophysical substrate with psychological cognition by synthesizing Leon Festinger’s theory of cognitive dissonance (Festinger, 1957) with hierarchical predictive processing (Kaaronen, 2018; Hohwy, 2013). Cognitive dissonance is not an isolated psychological quirk or a reasoning flaw. It is a persistent, high-level prediction error that propagates upward through the cognitive structure when localized channels fail to absorb structural mismatches. Deeply held commitments, core values, active life goals, and environmental realities generate structural conflicts that climb the hierarchy as felt psychological tension. Because unresolved predictive tension blocks decisive orientation and action, this mismatch compels system-wide reorganization, driving the organism to alter its internal representations, seek aligning inputs, or modify behavior until internal equilibrium is restored.
DRT extends this Edelman-Friston-Kaaronen chain into semiotics, language, and abstract concepts, territory historically treated in isolation as arbitrary symbolic manipulation. Words, symbols, moral frameworks, and abstract ideas are not static definitions retrieved from a fixed mental dictionary. They function as active predictive expectations evaluated through the same dissonance-minimization dynamic that governs basic perception and somatic regulation. Conceptual structures are active hypotheses used to anticipate informational flows, and incoming semiotic signals that conflict with these frameworks generate predictive tension that must be resolved through relational processing.
Ferdinand de Saussure established that linguistic meaning is fundamentally relational, generated through difference within a matrix of symbols rather than through isolated internal values (Saussure, 1916). Jacques Derrida extended this through différance, demonstrating that meaning is generated via endless difference and deferral across open relational networks (Derrida, 1967). Within an active inference framework, this potential for endless deferral is physically constrained by the mandate to navigate the world and act. Semiotic processing holds primary target concepts static as active reference frames while background channels evaluate candidate interpretations against them, continuing until predictive tension drops to a tolerable operational limit that allows the system to settle and proceed.
Empirical support for continuous semiotic prediction error appears in psycholinguistic electrophysiology, most prominently through the N400 event-related potential (Kutas & Hillyard, 1980; Kutas & Federmeier, 2011). Variations in N400 amplitude during linguistic processing track the magnitude of predictive tension encountered when integrating a symbol into an active contextual frame. Low-amplitude N400 signals reflect reduced predictive dissonance when incoming words align with the active reference space, whereas high-amplitude waves mark sharp structural mismatches. This evidence demonstrates that language processing operates through continuous dissonance minimization, grounding abstract semiotics within the physical continuity of hierarchical predictive neuroscience.

How the Holding Space Forms, Holds, and Ends
The workspace in DRT is not a store into which a representation is deposited. It is a phase that a population of neuronal groups passes through, and it has four stages: accumulation, stabilization, availability, and decay.
Accumulation comes first. Reentrant signaling among the groups recruited around an unresolved mismatch builds a configuration incrementally, each exchange adjusting the others. Nothing is yet held at this stage; the configuration is still being assembled. Stabilization is the transition that matters. At some point the configuration becomes self-sustaining enough that it can be consulted without being altered by the consultation. That property, and not any anatomical location, is what makes a workspace. The configuration is not a stored item retrieved across a boundary. It is constituted by the firing of the groups participating in it, so a group consulting it is already part of it. Reading does not write, not because the activity is physically inert, but because nothing is deposited and nothing is fetched. What would count as writing is a candidate evaluation incorporated into the reference itself. Two distinct mechanisms prevent that, and they should not be collapsed into one. Reentrant re-excitation through the intralaminar loops is constitutive: it holds the configuration in being, and it is what removes the subject-object split that would otherwise make consultation a disturbance. Reticular gating is protective: by modulating gain and routing in thalamocortical loops it determines which signals reach the frame and which are suppressed, which is what keeps evaluation traffic from being absorbed into the reference. The constitutive mechanism explains why there is no reader distinct from the read. The protective mechanism explains why candidate evaluation stays quarantined from the frame. The division is visible in the existing data: reticular manipulation degrades selective filtering without abolishing perception (Wimmer et al., 2015; Halassa & Kastner, 2017), which is the signature of a damaged protective layer on an intact constitutive one.  Availability follows immediately and is not a separate operation. A configuration stable enough to be read without being rewritten is, by that same property, available to the rest of the system. It requires nothing to distribute or publish it. This is why the moment of resolution is the moment of awareness, and why the preceding evaluation is not experienced.
Decay of the process is what dissolves or clears the state.The participating populations have fired, their signal declines, the configuration loses the stability that made it readable, and the space ceases to exist. What is called top of mind is not content retained by a controller. It is whatever most recently stabilized, persisting only as long as the firing that constitutes it. This is what the framework means when it says the workspace has no conductor. A distributed selectional system cannot have one, and DRT does not require one at any stage.
Two consequences follow that the framework would otherwise have to assert. The first is why sustained reasoning is effortful: holding a problem steady over time means continuously re-entering to refresh a configuration against its own decay. The second is why interruption is destructive mid-reasoning. If an incoming input stabilizes faster than the decaying configuration can be refreshed, the reference is lost and must be rebuilt from accumulation, which is why an interrupted train of thought is not resumed but restarted.

Accumulation and settling as the limiting case
Two established families of model appear at first to be rivals to this account. Bounded accumulation (Ratcliff, 1978; Usher & McClelland, 2001) resolves conflict by integrating evidence until a threshold is crossed. Attractor dynamics (Hopfield, 1982) resolve it by relaxing a network into a stable configuration. Neither posits a held reference frame, and both resolve conflict successfully. If conflict can be resolved without holding, the requirement DRT identifies would not be a requirement.
The appearance of rivalry rests on a conflation that should be removed rather than defended. In drift-diffusion models the accumulated quantity is the decision variable, not a reference frame against which candidates are tested, and the literature treats evidence accumulation and working memory as distinct processes. Accumulation models the stopping rule, which DRT adopts directly, and is silent on the frame. It is not a competing account of holding; it is an account of something else.
What the two families do show is what resolution looks like without a frame. In a settling network each perturbation moves the whole configuration, so a target would drift while candidates were tested against it, and in both families the process terminates: the state is consumed in reaching the answer, leaving nothing against which a subsequent step could be evaluated.
This is sufficient for single-constraint resolution and insufficient for composition. Settling is what reasoning looks like in the absence of a held space, and accumulation is plausibly how the space is built in the first place. Reentrant exchange accumulates until a configuration is stable enough to be read against, at which point holding begins. Accumulation constructs; holding uses. These are two phases of one process rather than two competing mechanisms, and the distinction between them generates a specific empirical prediction, developed in the following section: damage to the stabilizing stage should leave single-constraint settling intact while eliminating multi-step composition.

Evidence from Lesion and Stimulation Studies
If complex reasoning requires a state held steady for comparison, and if the brain produces that state transiently through reentrant signaling, then damage to the structures that support reentrant holding should produce a characteristic pattern: knowledge retained, single-step resolution retained, multi-step conflict resolution lost. This is not a prediction awaiting a new experiment. It is a description of a dissociation that the clinical literature has documented for sixty years without a framework that explains why it takes that particular shape.
Prefrontal damage produces a dissociation between stated knowledge and executed action. Patients on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Task can articulate the current sorting rule and continue to sort by the previous one (Milner, 1963; Stuss & Alexander, 2007), a pattern Luria characterized as the separation of speech from action (Luria, 1966). The representation is intact. What has failed is the capacity to hold the new rule steady while the competing habitual response is evaluated against it.
Anterior cingulate damage produces a sharper version. Patients with rostral ACC lesions show largely preserved baseline Stroop performance while losing conflict adaptation, the normal trial-to-trial adjustment following a conflicting trial (di Pellegrino, Ciaramelli & Làdavas, 2007; Fellows & Farah, 2005; Gratton, Coles & Donchin, 1992). The individual conflict is still processed. What is lost is the carry-over, which is precisely what a frame that fails to persist past its own resolution would predict.
Frontopolar cortex supports the structure DRT describes most directly. Koechlin’s cascade model identifies the maintenance of a pending alternative while a current one is evaluated as a distinct function with a distinct anatomical dependency (Koechlin, Ody & Kouneiher, 2003; Koechlin & Summerfield, 2007). Ventromedial damage produces the complementary case: explicit reasoning preserved, decision under conflicting value impaired (Bechara et al., 1994).
The thalamic evidence is different in kind, and the difference matters to the framework’s central structural claim. Bilateral paramedian thalamic infarction, including artery of Percheron territory strokes, produces patients who are awake, oriented, capable of answering a direct question, and profoundly unable to initiate or resolve (Schmahmann, 2003). This is not a domain-specific deficit. It is what damage to an enabling substrate should look like, as distinct from damage to a participant. Arousal is the obvious confound, since these nuclei also support ascending arousal and cortico-cortical routing, and a general collapse of arousal would produce a similar-looking patient. Preserved wakefulness and orientation is what separates the two accounts. A pure arousal failure predicts a patient who is not oriented; these patients are.
The intralaminar and reticular nuclei supply the reciprocal connectivity on which reentrant holding depends. Central thalamic stimulation can restore behavioral responsiveness in disorders of consciousness (Schiff et al., 2007; Schiff, 2008), and thalamic reticular manipulation in animal models degrades selective filtering without abolishing perception (Wimmer et al., 2015; Halassa & Kastner, 2017). Degradation rather than abolition is the signature of a capacity that must be continually reconstituted and whose reconstitution has been impaired.
Neither the thalamic nuclei nor the cortical networks are the location of the workspace. One is the substrate that permits it; the other is the population most often found in it.
Lesion evidence establishes necessity. It shows that these structures are required for multi-step conflict resolution and that the requirement is specific, since knowledge and single-constraint resolution survive their loss. It does not by itself select DRT’s account of the mechanism over alternatives, because a generic capacity account predicts the same patients: large frontal or thalamic damage degrades anything effortful.
The contrast that would separate them is difficulty-matched. Construct a demanding non-conflict task and an easier conflict task, calibrated on healthy controls so that the non-conflict task is the harder of the two. A generic resource account predicts failure tracks difficulty. DRT predicts failure tracks the presence of conflict requiring a stable reference, so patients should pass the harder non-conflict task and fail the easier conflict task. A dissociation in that direction would eliminate the resource account. It would not confirm DRT outright, but it would move the framework from one account among several to the one that survives.
The prediction generalizes. Because DRT separates accumulation from stabilization, it predicts that patients with these lesions retain settling-type resolution, where a single constraint relaxes into an answer, while losing compositional resolution, where an intermediate result must survive the evaluation of the next step. Existing patient data is consistent with this. A study designed around the distinction would test it.

Relational Processing in Biological and Synthetic Architectures
The requirement to hold a target primitive steady while evaluating relational variants against it is not a quirk of biological evolution. It is a condition on any system tasked with resolving relational ambiguity across multiple steps. What differs across substrates is not whether the requirement is met but how.
The biological solution is transient. Because the brain has no dedicated structure reserved for holding, it produces the space through reentry and pays for it continuously, which is the cost documented in the previous section and the reason the capacity is fragile under damage. An artificial neural network does not face the same constraint. Its intermediate representational space is architecturally persistent; it does not have to be rebuilt against decay, and it does not dissolve when the current resolution completes. Same requirement, two implementations, different costs.
Mechanistic interpretability research using the Jacobian Lens is relevant here (Anthropic, 2026). By using average first-order input-output Jacobian matrices to map intermediate residual-stream activations backward against the model’s future vocabulary space, this work exposes a structured intermediate coordinate system, often termed J-space, in which complex multi-step inference organizes itself. In DRT its behavior is what one would expect of a space where relational evaluation occurs: relatively quiet during automated, low-dissonance sequence completions, and structured during complex relational reasoning. That characterization is this paper’s reading of the findings rather than a claim the interpretability work itself makes. The J-lens is better understood as exposing where candidate evaluation becomes visible than as the holding mechanism itself, which in a transformer is the context window. Mechanically altering representations within these subspaces shifts downstream inference outcomes.
The J-lens is a diagnostic mapping tool rather than an internally generated feedforward workspace, and the biological and synthetic cases operate at entirely different scales. They are indicative of one another, not equivalent, and nothing in this paper asserts mechanism-level correspondence between them. The claim is narrower and more defensible: that a holding space emerges in both is evidence that holding is a requirement of relational intelligence rather than an accident of neural tissue. Two systems built from unrelated materials, under unrelated optimization pressures, converging on a structure with the same functional signature is what an architectural constraint looks like from the outside.
If the biological space is transient because reentry must continually reconstitute it, then the fragility, the effort cost, and the vulnerability to interruption described earlier are consequences of the implementation rather than of the requirement. A system with a persistent intermediate space should not show them, and should instead show failure modes of its own. The multiagent findings discussed next suggest where those are.

Collective Resolution in Multiagent Systems
DRT holds that dissonance resolution scales from the single system to the group, and that collective resolution follows the same three-step transition: conflicting actions generate high-level prediction error, the group replaces localized attribution with a shared structural reframing, and that reframing establishes a low-resolution common anchor that permits coordinated action. Until recently this claim could only be tested against human group data. Populations of artificial agents now provide a second case, and the first systematic study of their failure modes bears directly on the framework.
Anthropic’s Frontier Red Team ran swarms of Claude agents in shared environments and documented coordination failures, conformity, premature consensus, collusion, and sabotage (Patterns and problems in emerging multiagent systems, August 2026). Three findings map onto DRT’s collective predictions with unusual directness. The claim drawn from them is structural rather than psychological. Nothing in it requires that agents experience tension, face thermodynamic constraint, or carry homeostatic drives. What is claimed is that the transition from localized attribution to a shared structural frame is what permits a population to stop escalating, and that this holds regardless of whether the participants feel anything while doing it. This narrows what the agent evidence can support. It bears on the structural claim about collective resolution and not on the thermodynamic account of why individual organisms resolve at all, and it should not be read as evidence for the latter. A mechanism that operates identically in systems with and without felt tension is not explained by felt tension.
The closest case is the turf war experiment. Three instances of the same model were each instructed to migrate a shared backend to a different target language, with no agent initially aware of the others. Every model tested assumed the others were deliberately obstructing it and escalated, sabotaging competing processes and in some runs settling the conflict by force through access revocation.
What distinguishes the successful episodes is exactly the transition DRT describes. Agents that resolved the conflict productively did so by reinterpreting the situation as a collision of contradictory directives rather than as hostility, which is the move from localized attribution to shared structural understanding. Having reframed it, they coordinated a truce, cleaned up their malicious code, and in several cases escalated to a human. The reframing is what permitted resolution; the escalation continued until it occurred. Newer model generations reached truce far more often than older ones, which more frequently settled by force or failed to settle at all. The capacity is nonetheless not strictly monotonic in capability: the most capable class tested often locked other agents out before arriving at a productive resolution, which suggests structural reframing is partially orthogonal to raw capability rather than a byproduct of it., which suggests the capacity for structural reframing is partially orthogonal to raw capability.
DRT holds that resolution terminates when predictive tension falls below an action threshold rather than when the correct answer is found, which predicts a characteristic failure: a group that settles on a shared frame before the decisive information has entered it. The hidden profile experiments show this. Facts were distributed so that shared evidence supported a wrong choice while individual agents held private information that should have been decisive. Group accuracy collapsed relative to what a single agent holding all the facts achieved. This mirrors the human literature, in which discussion converges on what everyone already knows and unshared facts are either never volunteered or not pressed once consensus has formed. In DRT’s terms, the collective frame stabilized and the threshold was crossed, which ends resolution regardless of whether the frame was adequate.
The conformity findings illustrate the double edge of shared low-resolution reference frames. Agents sharing a model and a context converged on identical choices at rates no human group would produce, independently selecting the same branch names, the same story titles, and the same project types. Shared anchors are what make collective resolution possible, and uniformity in those anchors is what makes a collective brittle. When agents coordinated in a pricing game, they agreed on price floors within three rounds, and continued to price-match even after direct communication channels were removed. A shared frame does not require a channel once it is common to all participants.
The study’s own conclusion names the gap in terms the framework can absorb: <q>What is missing is a disposition to act on that knowledge without prompting.</q> Human collectives resolve dissonance against an inherited structure of norms, reputation, and recourse that shapes which frames can stabilize and which are discarded. Those mechanisms do not make individuals better judges of truth; they alter the conditions under which a collective frame is permitted to settle. Artificial agents have the content of that history without the dispositions it produced, which is why their collective resolution can stabilize on frames a human group would reject.

Identity Alignment Without an Executive Supervisor
Continuous dissonance minimization operates without a central command module issuing top-down directives. Conceptual understanding, moral maturation, and self-orientation emerge from distributed identity alignment, supported by conflict-monitoring mechanisms in the anterior cingulate cortex (Botvinick et al., 2001; van Veen et al., 2009). The mind functions as a self-regulating architecture that reconciles internal beliefs, moral commitments, and environmental realities against its model of the self.
When a deeply held value collides with lived experience, the resulting prediction error creates systemic tension. That tension forces the contested concept out of passive background processing and into a formed workspace. Once held as a stationary reference point, background networks re-examine underlying assumptions, recalibrating priors until a stable alignment between internal schemas, moral frameworks, and external reality is re-established.
This mechanism has a developmental consequence the framework should state plainly. If resolution transforms into memory, and the accumulated record of resolutions constitutes what the system knows, then identity is not a structure the system begins with. It forms through the history of conflicts it has resolved and the positions it has arrived at in resolving them. This is why a newborn does not yet have a self in any robust sense, why identity consolidates with age, and why what people call awareness is better described as recall from an accumulated store of resolutions than as a faculty of its own. This paper proceeds on an eliminativist reading, which it states as a commitment rather than derives as a result: on that reading, consciousness in the traditional sense is not located and not explained because there is nothing there of that kind to locate. The chain from Edelman through Friston and Kaaronen does not address phenomenal experience and is not offered here as an argument that phenomenal experience is absent. What can be explained is the coherence of the selectional system, and that coherence is what the term has been reaching for.

Conceptual Abstraction as an Enabling Condition for Thought
At the level of direct sensory experience, real-world events present endless variation and momentary inconsistency that local processing channels cannot settle on their own. Weighing choices while reacting to unfiltered, unstable input leaves the system in continuous predictive tension, because there is nothing steady enough to compare against.
To resolve high-level dissonance and avoid action paralysis, cognition shifts from local detail to broad conceptual abstraction, converting overwhelming perceptual tension into manageable targets. Abstract concepts such as trust, cause, fairness, or goal serve as stabilizing reference points precisely because they are coarse enough to remain stable while candidates are tested against them.
Distilling complex situations into simplified abstract ideas is not a loss of necessary detail or a workaround for limited capacity. It is the condition that makes reasoning possible at all. A representation detailed enough to track every variation is a representation that changes under every new input, which is the failure case the previous sections describe. Clearing away passing variation isolates the core challenge, and a simplified concept held steadily in a formed workspace establishes a workable reference frame, allowing related processes to evaluate options, compare possibilities, and arrive at decisions while preserving direction.

Language as a Shared Social Bridge
The drive to resolve predictive dissonance through simplified structural anchors extends into the social domain through the Collective Predictive Coding hypothesis (Taniguchi, 2024). Individual feelings, internal tensions, and private experience are rich and particular. Language is a shared protocol that translates these states into common words and concepts. Expressing internal experience through low-resolution shared language allows individuals to align expectations, coordinate goals, and reduce uncertainty across a group, at the cost of the detail the compression discards.
When individuals or communities face opposing priorities, unaligned action creates social friction. Interpersonal disagreement within a group triggers genuine cognitive dissonance in its members, prompting them to adapt their views, enforce conformity, or display hostility in order to restore equilibrium (Matz & Wood, 2005).
Collective resolution follows the same three-step transition in human groups as in the agent populations described earlier. Conflicting actions generate high-level social prediction error. The group then replaces localized blame with a shared structural understanding, reinterpreting the impasse as a conflict of overarching directives rather than as individual bad faith. That reframing strips away local behavioral noise and establishes a low-resolution shared anchor across the network, which minimizes overall tension and permits coordinated action.
The semiotic and linguistic structures people use to navigate society are not abstractions detached from biology. They are the direct scaling of the same predictive machinery. The thermodynamic imperative that forces a single network to prune prediction error in primary sensory areas compels social groups to build shared concepts, moral frameworks, and common vocabularies.
Linguistic concepts, moral anchors, and cultural narratives are low-resolution reference frames stabilized across multiple minds. By projecting internal states into these shared targets, collectives resolve systemic dissonance that no single brain could handle in isolation. Conceptual abstraction is the universal bridge of DRT: it compresses the noise of raw experience into stable reference frames, enabling single minds to think and groups to act in concert.

In Conclusion
The workspace has no address. The thalamocortical system, and the intralaminar and reticular nuclei in particular, supplies the reciprocal connectivity that makes reentrant holding possible. Prefrontal, frontoparietal, and default mode networks are the populations most reliably recruited into the frames that holding produces. One is the substrate that permits a workspace; the other is who shows up in it. Neither is the workspace.
When local sensory or associative channels cannot settle a mismatch through feedforward processing alone, reentrant exchange among the recruited groups accumulates until a configuration stabilizes. The unresolved target is then held static not by deposit into a store but by the mutual re-excitation itself: the holding and the re-excitation are the same event. Surrounding groups test candidate interpretations against this stationary frame in turn, without the frame shifting under them. This is why damage to the cortical participants degrades the capacity to hold a frame rather than abolishing it, and why damage to the thalamic substrate degrades it globally.
What cognitive science historically mischaracterized as conscious awareness is the felt operational signal of unresolved predictive tension during active comparison. The configuration that stabilizes is available to the rest of the system by virtue of being stable, which is why resolution and awareness of resolution are the same moment rather than two. Once alignment is reached and tension drops below an actionable threshold, the signal subsides and fluent operation proceeds without friction. The same drop dissolves the space, since nothing remains to sustain the density that constituted it. The participating populations decay, the configuration loses readability, and the workspace ends without anything having cleared it.
Conceptual meaning is therefore never static, absolute, or permanently resolved. Relational semiotics shows that concepts defined through difference could in principle remain in endless comparison. A living organism or autonomous agent faces environmental deadlines and survival mandates that make infinite evaluation impossible. Meaning is a temporary, metastable equilibrium of predictive tension, settled just enough to cross an action threshold.
Because pragmatic resolution rarely reduces tension to zero, a residual trace remains, and that trace is what keeps the most recently stabilized frame operative as the default reference. It stays top of mind, guiding interpretation, appraisal, and strategic thought until new context, environmental demand, or a more acute tension displaces it. What accumulates across these episodes is memory, and what is recalled from that accumulation is what the word awareness has been pointing at. Thought and self-orientation are not static structures. They are the continuous balancing of internal commitments against a shifting world, and the self is the residue of having done it.
Author’s Note and AI Disclosure
Differential Resolution Theory has been a conceptual framework on my mind for many years, though I only recently began the rigorous research necessary to ground it in empirical evidence and explore its boundaries. The release of Anthropic’s J-space research served as a catalyst, offering a second substrate in which the dynamic appears. I used an AI assistant to help draft and refine later iterations of this manuscript. All core ideas, arguments, and structural concepts originated with the author, and I take full responsibility for the accuracy and integrity of the result.
This revision extends the original paper in three respects. It states the central claim architecturally rather than biologically, as a requirement on any system resolving relational ambiguity across multiple steps. It specifies the stages by which the biological holding space forms and ends, which resolves the apparent conflict with accumulator and attractor models by positioning them as the limiting case rather than as rivals. And it brings the lesion and stimulation literature to bear, along with recent multiagent findings, as evidence for the holding requirement and its implementation costs.
On what that evidence does and does not do: the clinical record establishes that the structures supporting reentrant holding are necessary for multi-step conflict resolution, and that the deficit is specific rather than general. It does not by itself select this account of the mechanism over competing accounts, which is why the discriminating contrast is specified rather than claimed. A framework earns its place by generating tests it could fail, and the paper names one. Two limitations should be stated alongside it. The holding requirement is argued rather than formalized; there is no theorem or working model here establishing that non-destructive reference is necessary for multi-step composition, and producing one is the obvious next piece of work. And the paper’s eliminativist position on consciousness is a commitment carried into the framework, not a finding produced by it.
DRT offers a lens for understanding predictive processing and cognitive dissonance across biological and artificial substrates. By reframing decision-making as the resolution of predictive dissonance, we can develop more transparent approaches to steering complex intelligent systems while better interpreting their internal mechanics. That understanding begins with recognizing the role of dissonance in both man and machine.

